{"answer":"\\((x^2-4)/(x-2)=x+2\\) for \\(x\\ne2\\). The original expression has domain \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{2\\}\\). The simplified formula \\(x+2\\) is defined for every real number, so it is not equivalent to the original expression at every real number: at \\(x=2\\), the original is undefined whereas \\(x+2=4\\).","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the difference of squares and cancel the common factor only on the original domain.","id":"factor-cancel","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It directly establishes both the simplification and the excluded value."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies “For real x where the expression is defined”.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-expression","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The expression under consideration is (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) for real x where it is defined.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by factoring and cancellation in factor-cancel step.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-expression"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x with x != 2, (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) = x + 2.","step_ids":["factor-cancel"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the nonzero-denominator requirement in domain step.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-expression"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of the original expression is R \\ {2}.","step_ids":["domain-check"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by evaluating the simplified formula and comparing its domain with the original domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["simplification","domain"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"not-global-equivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The original expression and x + 2 are not equivalent at every real number, because the former is undefined at x = 2 while the latter equals 4 there.","step_ids":["comparison-at-two"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"domain-check","justification":"A quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero. Here x - 2 != 0 exactly when x != 2.","statement":"The original expression is defined exactly for real x != 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["domain-check"],"id":"factor-cancel","justification":"x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2). On the domain from domain-check, x - 2 is nonzero, so it may be cancelled.","statement":"For x != 2, (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) = ((x - 2)(x + 2))/(x - 2) = x + 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["domain-check","factor-cancel"],"id":"comparison-at-two","justification":"domain-check excludes x = 2 from the original expression. The polynomial x + 2 is defined at x = 2 and has value 4.","statement":"The simplified formula extends to x = 2, but the original expression does not; hence they are not equal as real-valued expressions on every real number."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
